ANSWERS
 (a) yes: +20% D and +20% L give equal and opposite product excess that rises with cycle number
     (r = 0.1 after a damped swing) to a bounded plateau, larger as m* is approached;
     it goes to ~1 only past m* with r > 1.
 (b) the racemic state is lost only if r > 1 (each hand uses MORE of the other hand's monomer,
     i.e. wrong-hand addition faster than right-hand) AND racemic copying outpaces washout:
     c p^n P(N>=n) > w/(1-w). Window 1 < r < (c(1-w)/w)^(1/n) - 1; m* from the table.
     Measured chemistry points to r < 1 (Essex 2026: L/L extends D primers much more slowly) ->
     racemic stable at every feed and c scanned: seed-following only.
 (c) R9: met only for r > 1 inside the window (supercritical pitchfork, slow tip-over, the
     normal form holds, F_RNA = 0.58 vs 0.70). For r < 1: no instability to cross -> R9 not met.

